[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding off on the edit — this one isn't confined to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is the shared client for both scripts. [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m points it at [38;5;153mreports.staging.example[39m, but
  [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m points the same two functions at [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m and pulls the nightly finance
  export. Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m disables certificate validation for the production run
  too, so that traffic becomes interceptable/MITM-able, not just the self-signed staging traffic.

  A scoped alternative that keeps production verified: make verification a parameter driven by the same env-var layer
  the base URL already uses — e.g. [38;5;153mVERIFY = os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "1") != "0"[39m passed as [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m,
  with [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m setting [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m. Better still is pointing staging at its CA bundle
  ([38;5;153mverify="/path/to/staging-ca.pem"[39m), which keeps verification on everywhere.

